// @check
// @error unknown type 'Zork'
// @warning Unused variable `z`
// CONTRACT (must-error), and the NEGATIVE CONTROL for D1590: a name with no plausible
// neighbour gets the bare `unknown type '<name>'` and no suggestion at all.
//
// A wrong guess is worse than none — it sends the reader to a type they did not mean — so the
// suggester offers a name only from the newcomer spelling map or from within an edit-distance
// budget that scales with length (1 up to 4 characters). `Zork` is four characters and sits 4
// edits from `null`, 5 from `Circle`; nothing is close enough and nothing is offered.
//
// THIS TIER CANNOT SEE THE ABSENCE. An `@error` directive matches by substring, so it would
// pass just as happily if a suffix appeared. The assertion that this message is EXACTLY
// `unknown type 'Zork'` lives in `tests/vl_unknown_type_suggestion_test.ts`; this file is what
// keeps the shape in the corpus, beside `unknown-type-did-you-mean.vl`'s three hits.
type Circle = { r: f64 }

const z: Zork = 1
print(0)
